# The endings module: how a call the runtime ran apart ended, as the code waiting on it reads it.
# An instance's end (§17.1) and a call into a context (§17.1) both reach their waiter as the run tells
# it, so the one reading of that lives here, outside the prelude, where no program sees it unasked.

# The `Result` an ending gives: `Ok` of what the call gave, alone in `given`; or, with nothing
# given, `Err` of its panic's message and trace.
fn resultOfEnding<T>(ending: (Array<T>, String, String)) -> Result<T>:
  const given, message, trace = ending
  if given.length() == 1: Ok(given[0])
  else: Err(Error{message, code: "panic", trace})
